 affordability narrative, where diesel costs and data-center politics have become the closing themes.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Tony Evers (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's governorship is open with Tony Evers retiring, and Milwaukee County executive David Crowley faces Congressman Tom Tiffany. Polling since midsummer has shown Crowley ahead by three to five points among likely voters, including surveys from Marquette, TIPP, GBAO and RMG, though the most recent fielded in late August. Prediction markets on Kalshi, Polymarket and PredictIt all price Crowley around 80-85 percent, more confident than the polls alone.\n\nExpert raters split between Tossup and Lean D. Tiffany has a decisive fundraising edge, out-raising Crowley roughly ten to one, which could fuel a strong closing push. The race has centered on affordability, diesel prices, and data centers, with Crowley tying Tiffany to rising costs. The state's even partisan lean and the open-seat uncertainty keep this competitive, but the weight of polling and market evidence favors Crowley modestly.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7127, "margin": 2.83}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.732, "margin": 3.0}]}